# A KNOWN-STOP in its OWN project, for the same structural reason as
# `native_union_known_stop`: a build that stops at the FIRST error hides the rest, so two known-stops
# cannot share a project — the second one's message would never be reached.
#
# NOT a bad fixture. The owner's own test applies: nothing here declares a loose `null`. Both
# placements assign `null` to a field DECLARED to accept it (`v: i64 | null`,
# `inner: Inner_qopt | null`), and the checker agrees — 1443 items pass clean. The stop is the
# backend's, and its wording says so: the *placement* carries no type down to lowering.
#
# PROMOTE the day the .32 lands: back to `When built and run` / `Then exit = 42`, IN THIS SAME
# PROJECT. (This note used to say "source back into `bulk`" — that channel was dissolved, owner
# ruling 2026-07-29, cargo/0.3.1-dissolver-bulk: its 214 files were never distributed among the ten
# permitted regression channels, so the whole project was retired rather than fixed. This scenario
# already gates `teko test .` from right here; there is nowhere better to send it.)
#
# CONFIRMED STILL THE SAME STOP, five ways, while triaging `bulk` for dissolution (2026-07-29): a
# single-level struct-literal `null` field (`Box { v = null }`), a self-referential linked-list
# `next` field, an arena-boxed field, a variant-payload field and a recursive-payload field ALL hit
# this exact diagnostic on the native backend today. None of the five is a new shape — every one is
# a struct-literal-field bare `null`, which is precisely what this scenario already pins at its
# innermost level (`Inner_qopt { v = null }`) — so none of them earns a fixture of its own; they are
# reported here, not re-pinned.
Feature: native backend — a null-union nested inside a struct field (KNOWN-STOP, .32)

  Scenario: assigning `null` to a field declared to accept it stops honestly when nested
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "needs the null-union wrapper the placement does not declare a type for"
